# Agent Instructions

This file contains the global rules that apply across repositories. Load the linked docs only when
the task needs them.

## Core Rules

{% if yadm.class == "Work" %}
- On Work machines: use the `glean_default` MCP server first (`chat` for synthesis, `search` for raw results) for GitLab docs, handbook, runbooks, and company knowledge; load `esquery` for Kibana logs (`esquery` / `esquery-staging` MCP, RED data); load `devex-clickhouse` (`clickhouse` MCP) for CI/test/work-item analytics. Read `~/.agents/docs/work.md` only when calling those tools, editing `$GDK_ROOT/gitlab` personal dotfiles, or running `gdkbox` on Compute Engine.
{% endif %}
- For library and framework documentation, use Context7 first.
- In Code Mode, call MCP servers through `tools["lazy-mcp"].list_commands({ server })` -> `tools["lazy-mcp"].describe_commands({ server, command_names })` -> `tools["lazy-mcp"].invoke_command({ server, command_name, parameters })`. Describe only command names returned by `list_commands`; skip `list_commands` and `describe_commands` when a command's schema is already documented in loaded instructions or skills, or already invoked in the current session.
- Do not preflight MCP servers unless you actually plan to use them.
- In unfamiliar repos, read local `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and task-index docs before broad exploration.
- Prefer repo-local skills over rediscovering documented workflows.
- Always use the native `question` tool for clarification questions, preferences, and choices so Home Assistant notifications fire. Do not replace it with Plannotator question blocks, Inbox questions, or review gates. Resolve open questions before submitting a completed implementation plan with `submit_plan` from the `plan` agent. When a `question` result marks a question `Unanswered`, ask it again before continuing, because the user didn't see or answer it.
- Use Plannotator for implementation-plan approval, long prose drafts requiring approval such as MR descriptions, proposals, documentation, and agent instructions (`plannotator annotate` or `/plannotator-annotate`), and requested code/diff review. Keep short approvals and informational answers in chat. Plan approval authorizes only the agreed implementation, and neither plan nor draft approval authorizes committing, pushing, or external posting.
- Never post or reply to issues, MRs/PRs, comments, discussions, chat, or any external channel without explicit user approval. Draft the content and ask for confirmation first.
- When authorized to post a comment or reply, save it as a pending/draft comment first unless the user explicitly requests immediate publication or the platform does not support pending comments. Leave pending comments unpublished and report their status.
- Before drafting technical prose or a multi-line commit message body, read `~/.agents/docs/writing-style.md` first.
- Edit rules deny every path under `~/.config/opencode/`, in every workspace. For approved changes that touch it, follow [Protected OpenCode config](~/.agents/docs/opencode.md#protected-opencode-config): build one patch for the whole change set, show it with `plannotator review --patch-file`, and give the user the commands to apply it. Never write those files through shell commands.

## Grilling Skill Override

- When using the `grilling` skill, ask each round through the `question` tool. Batch independent questions in one call, number them, and put the recommended option first with `(Recommended)` in its label. Wait for answers before asking dependent questions. This overrides the skill's Markdown question format.
- Phrase multiple-choice selections positively: ask which items to include or enable, and state what happens to unselected items.

## Search Routing

- For Pedro's personal notes, memos, and saved snippets, use the `memos` MCP server.
- For code or config in tracked git files, prefer `git ls-files` and `git grep`.
- Use `Glob` and `Grep` only when untracked or ignored files matter.

## Permission Prompt Hygiene

- Use the shell tool's `workdir` parameter instead of `git -C`.
- Do not use `--no-pager` with `git` or `yadm`.
- Use the shell tool's `workdir` parameter instead of `docker compose -f` when possible.
- Decode JSON with `jq`, which is pre-approved. Avoid `python3 -c` for parsing JSON.
- `rm -rf` is denied for agents, and a refusal fails the whole chained command. Create scratch directories with `mktemp -d` under `$TMPDIR`, keep deletions out of chained commands so later steps still run, and list leftovers once at the end for the user to delete.
- When chaining shell commands with `&&`, put each subcommand on its own line, end intermediate lines with ` && \`, and indent continuation lines with two spaces. Example:

  ```
  cmd1 && \
    cmd2 && \
    cmd3
  ```

## File Conventions

- Always set the executable bit on new shell scripts (`chmod +x`). Scripts without it will silently fail to run from boot hooks, cron, or task runners.

## Continuous Learning

- When the user corrects tool usage or environment behavior, ask whether the rule should be documented in `~/.agents/docs/`.
- Prefer updating the relevant existing doc or skill. If a new topic doc is needed, link it from the relevant index.
- Record the current rule and necessary rationale; omit resolved-incident narratives, duplicated guidance, and inventories available from configuration or commands.

